Namespace Commands 
----------------------------------------
Q1: 1. How to see the namespaces on the cluster?
We use the command: # kubectl get namespaces
This command displays all available namespaces in the cluster along with their status and age.

Q2: How do you create a namespace in Kubernetes?
We use: kubectl create namespace <namespace-name>
This command creates a new namespace to logically separate resources inside the cluster.
# kubectl create namespace dev

Q3: How do you create a namespace using YAML?
We define a Namespace object in a YAML file and apply it using kubectl.
This approach is preferred in real-world DevOps because it supports Infrastructure as Code (IaC) and version control.

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
 name: prod
 labels:
  env: production
  team: devops
  
kubectl apply -f namespace.yaml
kubectl get ns
kubectl describe ns prod
kubectl get ns --show-labels

Q4: How do you describe a namespace in Kubernetes?
We use: kubectl describe namespace <namespace-name>
This command provides detailed information about a specific namespace, including labels, annotations, resource quotas, and events.

Q5: How do you delete a namespace in Kubernetes?
We use: kubectl delete namespace <maspace-name>
This command deletes the entire namespace along with all resources (pods, services, deployments, etc.) inside it.

kubectl detete ns dev
Delete using YAML: kubectl delete -f namespace.yaml
kubectl get ns

Q6: How do you get all resources in a specific namespace?
We use: kubectl get all -n dev
This command lists all major resources (pods, services, deployments, replica sets, etc.) in the given namespace

kubectl get pods -n dev
kubectl get svc -n dev

Q7: What is kubectl apply -f app.yaml -n <namespace-name>?
         (or)
 How to create pod in specipic name space 
we use kubectl apply -f app.yaml -n <namespace-name>
This command is used to create or update Kubernetes resources defined in a YAML file inside a specific namespace.
The -n flag ensures the resources are deployed to the target namespace instead of default.

pod.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
 name: nginx-pod
 labels:
  app: nginx
spec:
 containers:
 - name: nginx-container
  image: nginx:latest
  ports:
  - containerPort: 80


 Create the Pod : kubectl apply -f pod.yml
 kubectl apply -f pod.yaml -n <namespace-name>
 kubectl get pods
 kubectl get all -n default
 
 kubectl get pods -A
 
 
Q8: How do you check the current namespace in Kubernetes?
We use the command kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace to check the current active namespace.
It shows the namespace set in the current context.
If no namespace is set, Kubernetes uses the default namespace.
This helps ensure commands are executed in the correct namespace.

# kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace

Q9: How do you switch from one namespace to another in Kubernetes?
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=dev
kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace
